INT. COLD-STORAGE LOCKER, FISH MARKET - NIGHT

Steel racks. Crates of ice. Breath fogging. A swinging bulb.

Two figures wedge the door shut with a pry bar.

DELPHINE "DEL" OKONKWO-REYES (30s, rubber apron) shoves a crate against it.

TOBIAS "TOBY" HARLAN-AMADI (50s, flannel, grizzled) leans on the bar, panting.

Something THUDS on the other side of the door. Once.

DEL
(to Toby)
Is it holding?

TOBY
It's holding.

THUD. The door shivers.

DEL
(to herself)
Okay. Okay. Don't scream.

TOBY
(to the whole locker)
Nobody make a sound.

DEL
Who are you talking to?

TOBY
Habit.

Silence. Ice drips. The bulb sways.

DEL
How long?

TOBY
Till morning crew.

DEL
That's six hours.

TOBY
Then we wait six hours.

Another THUD. Dents the steel. Del backs away. Her heel hits a crate. Cold light flickers across her face.

DEL
(whispering, to herself)
It's only the compressor.

TOBY
That's not the compressor.

The bulb goes out. Darkness. Breathing. A slow, wet scraping from the far end of the locker.

From the dark: a voice, gentle.

SABINE (O.S.)
Del?

Del freezes. Toby grabs her sleeve.

TOBY
(mouthing)
Don't.

SABINE (O.S.)
Del, it's me. Open the door.

DEL
(whispering)
Sabine?

The bulb flares. At the end of the aisle, in the white fog, stands SABINE ROUSSEAU-ADEKUNLE (30s, same apron as Del, hair wet, lips blue). She smiles. Her feet do not touch the floor.

Del makes a small sound. Toby heaves the pry bar up like a club.

SABINE
Oh, don't be afraid. I've come to warm up.

She drifts forward. The ice on the racks cracks. Frost races up the walls.

TOBY
(to Sabine)
Stay back!

DEL
(to Toby)
She's dead! She drowned in March!

SABINE
Del.

She extends a hand. Del's breath stops. Toby swings the bar. It passes through Sabine and rings against a steel rack. Sabine looks at him, hurt.

SABINE (CONT'D)
That wasn't kind.

The THUD on the door comes a third time and the door bursts open on the rush of morning crew: sunlight, shouting. Sabine vanishes in the light like breath on glass.

Toby drops the bar. Del falls to her knees. The ice on the racks begins, quietly, to melt.
